{"title":"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Mass_Points)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Mass Points)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Ceva_Theorem,_Height_to_Base_Relationships)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Ceva Theorem, Height to Base Relationships)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As shown in the figure, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Triangle\" title=\"Triangle\">triangle</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> is divided into six smaller triangles by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Line\" title=\"Line\">lines</a> drawn from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vertex\" title=\"Vertex\"> vertices</a> through a common interior point. The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Area\" title=\"Area\">areas</a> of four of these triangles are as indicated. Find the area of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AIME_1985_Problem_6.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/f/f7/AIME_1985_Problem_6.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"221\" height=\"173\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the interior point be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, let the points on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/b/fbb0f1d8a43539dbc3355734bafb2bfd855b5f87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CA}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, respectively.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> be the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/3/4f35ac29eb74c7d0efcac3195b41aa978e772ef9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle APE$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/4/b243d29f35d19841f53b0d6f9fa338171ffefeb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle CPD$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" />.  Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/c/8ac24ef6bb014c52b90e4e0a6eb28941a0577e2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle APF$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/c/24c7c8ba90199a3177d2d366d004bed0f6478fe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BPF$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> share the same <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Altitude\" title=\"Altitude\">altitude</a> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, so the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ratio\" title=\"Ratio\">ratio</a> of their areas is the same as the ratio of their bases.  Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/b/22bbdaf51a8a1faae81886d50358cbaf053ae08d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ACF$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/c/6acd42ef30a6319e5b63f48ae3e8a11120e56f63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BCF$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> share the same altitude from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, so the ratio of their areas is the same as the ratio of their bases.  Moreover, the two pairs of bases are actually the same, and thus in the same ratio.  As a result, we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/e/29ebb705c2b3223c3cb5e073ad1577f2f2158aaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{40}{30} = \\frac{124 + x}{65 + y}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"108\" height=\"41\" /> or equivalently <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/8/0d89eb161847b407976f18b1c5a8a1cc9724b5ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$372 + 3x = 260 + 4y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"160\" height=\"16\" /> and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/a/a9abe2981a5c2c49b1aff7a1e85c0fb44755574a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4y = 3x+ 112$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Applying identical reasoning to the triangles with bases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/0/ce0bfc603f9a044e308b1a1fdadca917e81ad2dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/7/4373e7b4be983c0315e82e1fda1df66ed34dd7fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BD}$\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c20a2976cfcf60dd0bed23f305d87cde065ed79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{y}{35} = \\frac{x+y+84}{105}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"131\" height=\"38\" /> so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/c/b5c092200a93bd8ba5b74c6e00d80297b6bcb753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3y = x + y + 84$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"125\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/0/7507c1a1b164b69bc6d3c81281df0435fc7d5628.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2y = x + 84$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"93\" height=\"16\" />.  Substituting from this equation into the previous one gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/2/1b23d3a285adaa848ed7c45ee82ad5280705cb63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 56$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />, from which we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/e/95ee373ccc06a39e5ddd4dc03452eacf3085e042.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 70$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"16\" /> and so the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/0/5f0e3ed69c0820358b79676e79d8db14a2dcaf64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$56 + 40 + 30 + 35 + 70 + 84 = \\Rightarrow \\boxed{315}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"301\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Mass_Points.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Mass_Points)\">Solution 2 (Mass Points)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Mass Points)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/9/c89681d39c76aa245c269497ced477e39a7aa8d8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(8cm); import graph;  // Define the vertices pair A = (0,0); pair B = (8,0); pair C = (6,6);  // Mass assignments real mA = 3; real mB = 4; real mC = 2;  // Edge points (D between B and C, E between A and B, F between A and C) // Formula: Point = (m1*P1 + m2*P2) / (m1 + m2) pair D = (mB*B + mC*C) / (mB + mC); // Mass 6 pair E = (mA*A + mB*B) / (mA + mB); // Mass 7 pair F = (mA*A + mC*C) / (mA + mC); // Mass 5  // Intersection (Center of Mass) pair P = (mA*A + mB*B + mC*C) / (mA + mB + mC); // Mass 9  // Draw the main triangle and cevians draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(1pt)); draw(A--D, gray); draw(B--F, gray); draw(C--E, gray);  // Dots for all key points dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(E); dot(F); dot(P, red);  // Labels with masses label(&quot;$A(3)$&quot;, A, SW); label(&quot;$B(4)$&quot;, B, SE); label(&quot;$C(2)$&quot;, C, N); label(&quot;$D(6)$&quot;, D, NE); label(&quot;$E(7)$&quot;, E, S); label(&quot;$F(5)$&quot;, F, NW); label(&quot;$P(9)$&quot;, P, 1.5*E, deepblue);  // Optional: Label the internal area ratios if needed // based on the original image style [/asy]\" width=\"378\" height=\"272\" />\nWe can solve this problem using <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mass_points\" title=\"Mass points\">mass points</a>. First, denote the interior point as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, the foot of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cevian\" title=\"Cevian\">cevian</a> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/9/1090530ae883b627e090bfb5e450ef796febca40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"16\" />, the foot of the cevian from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/7/4c71f19552a05ee5e2b31e94aff3fb986884bf31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B&#39;$\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, and the foot of the cevian from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/7/9b798193cb94562989018728e1151587c618655b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"16\" />. Notice how triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/a/ecaf4744d94107dff617115ccbcb8bc53c18bd01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta APC&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/4/a740615153d3b18561d6e2feee9ce423983e6c03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta BPC&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"16\" /> share the same <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Altitude\" title=\"Altitude\">altitude</a> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Because their areas are in the ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/7/8178571f09867e8b0e9835c1e0574563db663da6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40:30=4:3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"111\" height=\"14\" />, then their bases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/d/e2d3fcc93e75c0e9b61822ff041467f47f9c5bfe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC&#39;$\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/c/5bc856e7f29652b2c928f2c025ed9befbab1873e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC&#39;$\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> must also be in the ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/f/def7726c2babb527f7ab2ae4c436cc012ebbd351.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4:3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"13\" />. Next, notice how triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/f/b1f73d4ea8a1680d5316de0381596cd5638407f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta APB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/2/6c25c7edbb8fd9fea9f9892d77bc14c877556f54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta PBA&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" /> also share the same altitude from B due to the fact A,P,A' are collinear. Their areas are in the ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030919dfe45e82705f9f247cebcd059def804158.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40+30:35=70:35=2:1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"227\" height=\"15\" />, so the ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/5/0a5180163a3195581aed01569c5df228f95488a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP:PA&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" /> must also be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/3/0b3714d1977e2e6fa2c03536bd04eabab371fd18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2:1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>Now, we can apply mass points. First, assign vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> a weight of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Then, we determine the weight of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/d/f4d50fb68431993266b44c0454915058420c9915.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BC&#39;}{AC&#39;}\\cdot W_{B}=\\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"119\" height=\"40\" />, and by adding the weights of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/9/4f926bb541a5d90e90db72c18681a6012108509a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W_{C&#39;}=\\frac{7}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"72\" height=\"38\" />. Applying the same to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/e/6cee29e8c46b0074ecb11ec9eed51e26715a850c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AA&#39;}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" />, we get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/b/afbcb2d1820ef97cacf2dbb4504fb7c32022810e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W_{A&#39;}=\\frac{3}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"72\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/c/6ac4104fb865b86cf8838696ef5f5c25ec49d044.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W_{P}=\\frac{9}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/0/8b011ab9b65daf168255de69d84843cb3e67e9f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W_{C}=\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/5/1d5046746290a9f001f3c3a318b8f3535df896f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W_{B&#39;}=\\frac{5}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"72\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Finally, we can determine the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6d5d23a7e91fb9f6c248fb4cd43111165540f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta ABC$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" /> by adding together the areas of all of the small triangles, and using side length ratios derived from mass points to determine the areas of the two unknown triangles.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/0/9c0cfd30a5af51ac2ead2490d5c03e6272ccd4fc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[\\Delta ABC]=40+30+35+84+[\\Delta APB&#39;]+[\\Delta CPA&#39;]\\]\" width=\"416\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/e/efe2ead21612d7669201fca156f86c95957e798a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[\\Delta ABC]=189+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [\\Delta CPB&#39;]+2\\cdot [\\Delta BPA&#39;]\\]\" width=\"354\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/e/41e20665c29333c6b49fb7c630e497ce4f2fbd67.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[\\Delta ABC]=189+56+70\\]\" width=\"197\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/e/f3e4bf4e9d1531806ec9806ca328c4d85bccbc7a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[\\Delta ABC]=\\boxed{315}\\]\" width=\"129\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>~ Danny Wang (ThePeeps191)\n</p><p>~ Diagram By Daniel Wang (fp77)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the interior point be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and let the points on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/b/fbb0f1d8a43539dbc3355734bafb2bfd855b5f87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CA}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, respectively. Also, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/a/adaacb718fe2bbe09f434e15453291f1b5f8d658.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[APE]=x,[CPD]=y.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"190\" height=\"20\" /> Then notice that by Ceva's, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/d/f0d9f05753ec07861f0d1a0cadb239b1d00e1651.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{FB\\cdot DC\\cdot EA}{DB\\cdot CE\\cdot AF}=1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"154\" height=\"38\" /> However, we can deduce <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/a/97a2bbe82c960233aa9e735e4138a3acaaa25df8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{FB}{AF}=\\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"68\" height=\"38\" /> from the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/1/b61b01bca9ca7726d085f1a3e81250b4037b7e7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[AFP]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8cc258ba4a8e9e1022d7909533dfd6180eb745cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[BPF]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"51\" height=\"18\" /> share the same height. Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/e/dbe53444b94110f90792b4d5ccfd288e00ce6e1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac{84CE}{EA}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"83\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651875a3aa571c63cf9f8ab8544a1c05c0a7d490.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=\\frac{35DC}{BD}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"89\" height=\"37\" /> Plug and chug and you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/f/d4fd97119928eedffd01d609b391ea81bcc6b032.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy=84\\cdot 35\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=2205.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"183\" height=\"38\" /> Then notice by the same height reasoning, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/1/7c17e5d5f048bfe99f6758e7d50f36850ca429b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{84}{x}=\\frac{119+y}{x+70}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"113\" height=\"39\" /> Clear the fractions and combine like terms to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/1/8e18478a943b50a7cb858cbb68c509cfa153d4d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$35x=5880-xy.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"135\" height=\"16\" /> We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/3/d937f8fccc453498becf5a09d8943905b2c2ed10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy=2205$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"16\" /> so subtraction yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/5/cb591aff70266cf61e6d1edc6c3e17af74be7cfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$35x=3675,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"93\" height=\"16\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/2/d32797d6ef3580070a2d9867278bce5b2aeffe63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=105.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"65\" height=\"13\" /> Plugging this in to our previous ratio statement yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/7/837980ba0430e740b8dcd91f29136ad9afdbd6c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{84}{105}=\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{119+y}{175},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"160\" height=\"38\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/6/55617f4866ebe039a95ca66fec8caa6cacbc2986.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=21.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" /> Basic addition gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/9/129f79330f631c214954877ff2bef9e8b09e7dcb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$105+84+21+35+30+40=\\boxed{315}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"295\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>-dchen\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the interior point be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and let the points on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/b/fbb0f1d8a43539dbc3355734bafb2bfd855b5f87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CA}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, respectively. Then the cevians <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/8/478a0d4bf4aae9eb0b95f6a7a7f69b0e48dd6751.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD,BF,CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"16\" /> are concurrent, so we can use Ceva's Theorem, letting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/a/cda179e5e550c8851c47362c90f9c7dfc4146343.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BD}{DC}=\\frac{a}{b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"74\" height=\"39\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/a/2fa88f19ce162f61458417d2ca0f173ebb174142.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{CF}{FA}=\\frac{c}{d}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"72\" height=\"39\" />. Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/c/fcc13f9cda070155bab498f6e30031c3b5f9e6e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AE}{EB}=\\frac{[\\Delta APE]}{[\\Delta EPB]}=\\frac43.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"174\" height=\"45\" /> \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/5/b8572f318da4b158a1c429e66b77fc487cea8208.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{a}{b}\\cdot \\frac{c}{d}=1\\implies \\frac{d}{c}=\\frac{a}{b}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}.\\]\" width=\"228\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/5/a75c8754997d3d2fb0e2eb299bb01793df6728b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[\\Delta CPD]=35\\cdot \\frac ba$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"39\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/7/137e2ae01b70d49219df6e01e6654c68501802fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[\\Delta APF]=84\\cdot \\frac dc,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"143\" height=\"39\" /> so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/c/b9c86bb54f24b1d34516590c0c22dc861b963fed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[\\Delta ABC] = 84+84\\cdot \\frac dc + 35\\cdot \\frac ba + 40+30+35 = \\left(1+\\frac ba\\right)(40+30+35).\\]\" width=\"505\" height=\"43\" />\nWe will now solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/2/c022f88d3a2281464e7c2d1418e6f8d999fd3799.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac ba$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"12\" height=\"37\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/4/624685928d7da1022831a4dd498d4ced9fdb9250.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[84+84\\cdot \\frac ab\\cdot \\frac 43 + 35\\cdot \\frac ba = \\frac ba\\cdot 105.\\]\" width=\"259\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/7/94782041c891026b95579a4eea6a38dff5b29ea1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[12+16\\cdot \\frac ab + 5\\cdot \\frac ba = \\frac ba\\cdot 15\\]\" width=\"210\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/9/539eee4b68692696b0de2f8eb067d2e67cfad86a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[10\\left(\\frac ba\\right)^2-12\\cdot \\frac ba-16=0\\]\" width=\"207\" height=\"46\" />\nFactoring this gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/b/b1bc6b8383d6f9588a8d4171a1559d643735757b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac ba-2\\right)\\left(10\\cdot \\frac ba - 8\\right)=0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"217\" height=\"45\" /> so the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/1/cc13003d64b69c29f972221c20966cacb23b2666.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(1+\\frac ba\\right)(40+30+35)=3\\cdot 105=\\boxed{315.}\\]\" width=\"325\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>~ RubixMaster21\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Ceva_Theorem.2C_Height_to_Base_Relationships.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Ceva_Theorem,_Height_to_Base_Relationships)\">Solution 5 (Ceva Theorem, Height to Base Relationships)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Ceva Theorem, Height to Base Relationships)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>That common interior point screams Ceva’s theorem. However, we have no idea how to apply it yet! Let the common interior point be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. It follows that if the cevians from A, B, C, to the opposite sides intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/7/9575e1cb7b18dce01c064023128c9fd164ab5d9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D,E,F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> respectively, then triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/a/fda209d01cc013c21a16ccacace51f8d5bb84916.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$APF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/b/88be7975bfd01f490acc063b89d855c90d405f04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FPB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> share a height, meaning the ratio of their bases is the ratio of their areas, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/5/1d5874779af1a9d86e57b005fe1773db2d078e90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{40}{30}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" />. Let the area of triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/4/b34d84c9e3d325680e5479b88aca22cfb4d522ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$APE$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/a/f0a0a4554dcbef4be00a35d49eff85301adc911b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PCD$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. Then, notice that triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/9/419dea12de38c0f719dc92765204a7ccb24cd27f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AFC$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/a/3ca61b798add37d72a65243434f22b0f22cbb0b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FCB$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> share a height, so the ratio of their areas is the same as the ratio of their bases. The ratio of their bases however is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/5/1d5874779af1a9d86e57b005fe1773db2d078e90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{40}{30}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" />, so the ratio of their areas is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/f/b9ffd7142a432778aa4eaba9daf0dd6158ea8b69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{124+x}{65+y} = \\frac{40}{30}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"112\" height=\"42\" />. Now understand that the ratio of the remaining sides is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/e/68e335164897cb2d6d2d7b7de4390ad14645af1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{35}{y}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"24\" height=\"42\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/2/b72a4ad1b930d4137aff40052c4e43f587d5beb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{84}{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" />, so through Ceva’s Theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/9/269945eed2db02cd7739d27300e3e6a6a39b9eab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4 \\cdot 35 \\cdot 28}{ x\\cdot y} = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"111\" height=\"42\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/4/d04b2dbcdef739d53fbaa93e334bc9479082875b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy = 3920$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"82\" height=\"17\" />. From the above relation we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/f/b9ffd7142a432778aa4eaba9daf0dd6158ea8b69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{124+x}{65+y} = \\frac{40}{30}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"112\" height=\"42\" />, so a tad bit of algebra gives us two possible ordered pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" />, either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/6/7e6a31da8d9cdab29a5cc959c906c7a37533ce8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(56,70)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/f/1cfab206e4c410535886c7d57af5c88faa7435fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-\\frac{288}{3}, -42)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"102\" height=\"38\" />. Obviously, the negative area is nonexistent in Plane Euclidean Geometry. Therefore, the first ordered pair is true, and the area is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/6/536da3b1d1c5c7b5e1e8170383ac6985b6a219f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$56+70+84+40+30+35 = \\boxed{315}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"285\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/5jwD5UViZO8?t=300\">https://youtu.be/5jwD5UViZO8?t=300</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note\">Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This problem gives redundant information. The area of any one of the 4 triangles given can be derived from the other three triangles' areas\n</p><p>~ inaccessibles\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME\" title=\"1985 AIME\">1985 AIME </a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1985 AIME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=1985\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"1985 AIME Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1985_AIME_Pro